      title: 'The Recall Notice',
      body: 'Deacon Lionel Barnes ran the only auto repair shop on Ashby Street, and every Wednesday night he taught the men’s Bible class at Greater Mount Olive. One Wednesday he came in holding a letter from a car company. "Recall notice," he said, and passed it around. "The maker found a bad part in a brake line. They wrote to every owner. They pay for the fix. They did not wait for somebody to get hurt." Then he picked up an old study Bible that had belonged to his late uncle. The margins were full of notes, and one of them, he said, had taught his uncle something about people that the Bible never taught. "My uncle was a good man," Lionel said. "But he trusted a note more than the verse it sat under." A young brother named Marcus asked what you do with a book like that. Throw it out? Lionel shook his head. "You do two things. You honor anybody who admits a mistake, because that is rare and it is right. And you fix it where it was printed." He read them the verse that runs the whole recall business: "whoso confesseth and forsaketh them shall have mercy" (Proverbs 28:13). Then he handed each man a sticky note. "Go home tonight. Find one thing you taught your children or said at the barbershop that you know now was wrong. Write the fix. Then tell them." The next Wednesday the men came back with their notes. One had told his son he was wrong about a neighbor. One had called his sister. Marcus had written on his: "I said some people were just like that. The Bible says one blood." Lionel stuck all the notes on the board under the recall letter and wrote a heading over them in marker: Fixed at our expense.',
