      body: 'Pastor Evelyn Stokes found the letter while cleaning out her father’s church office after his funeral. It was from a publisher, thirty years old, apologizing for a lesson book the church had used in its Sunday school. The book had taught children that the races were meant to live apart. Her father had kept the apology in a drawer and never read it to the congregation. She sat a long time with it. Then she called the board together. "This letter is a gift," she said. "Somebody who had every reason to defend that book wrote down that it was wrong. But the book was taught out loud for years, and the apology sat in the dark." One of the older mothers, Sister Hattie, remembered the lessons. She remembered sitting in class as a girl and wondering whether Yahweh loved her less. Her voice shook as she said it. Pastor Stokes did not hurry past it. She read them the words of Zacchaeus, who did not stop at feeling sorry: "if I have taken any thing from any man by false accusation, I restore him fourfold" (Luke 19:8). The next Sunday she read the publisher’s letter from the pulpit. She did not shame her father, who had loved that church and served it forty years. She simply told the truth: the book was wrong, the apology was real, and it should have been read long ago. Then she asked Sister Hattie to come forward, and the whole church laid hands on her and prayed. After service the children’s teachers boxed up the last copies of the old book. They did not burn them in anger. They wrote a note inside each cover, naming the error and the verse that answers it, and kept one copy in the church library so no one would forget.',
