      title: 'The Question at Thanksgiving',
      body: 'Every Thanksgiving the Whitfield family played a game after dessert: everyone had to answer one hard question honestly. This year Grandpa Otis drew the card. It read: "How would you feel if your grandchild married someone of another race?" The room got quiet. Otis was eighty-one. He had grown up in Mississippi, had marched in Memphis, and had a long memory of what some people of other races had done. He looked around the table. His granddaughter Simone was home from nursing school, and everyone knew she had been spending time with a young man from her church named Daniel, whose parents came from Korea. Otis took a long breath. "I want to say the wrong answer," he admitted, and everybody laughed, and that broke the tension. "But I have been reading my Bible, and I cannot find the wrong answer in it." He picked up his worn King James from the side table and read the verse he had underlined: "Be ye not unequally yoked together with unbelievers" (2 Corinthians 6:14). "That is the line," he said. "Not color. Is he saved? Does he love the Lord? Does he treat her right?" Simone said yes to all three. Otis nodded slowly. "Then my fear is my problem, not his. I will work on it." His wife, Mother Ruth, squeezed his hand. Later that evening Otis called Simone aside. "Bring that young man to church with us at Christmas," he said. "I want to hear him pray." At Christmas Daniel prayed over the family meal, and Otis said amen louder than anyone. Afterward he told Daniel, "Son, you passed my test. Now I have to pass the Lord’s." Daniel did not fully understand, but Simone did, and she cried a little in the kitchen.',
