      body: 'In 1971 Bernice Coleman married a white man named Thomas Hale in a small chapel in Ohio. Four years earlier it would have been a crime in sixteen states. Bernice’s family came. Thomas’s family did not. His father wrote him a letter saying the Bible forbade it, and quoted a verse about the bounds of habitation. Thomas kept that letter in his Bible for fifty years. Now Bernice was a widow, and her granddaughter Kendra, a youth leader at New Hope Fellowship, asked her to speak to the teenagers about marriage. Bernice brought the letter. She read it to them, all of it, and the room was very still. Then she opened her Bible to the same chapter the letter had misused and read the whole verse: "hath made of one blood all nations of men" (Acts 17:26). "My father-in-law cut off the first half of the verse and kept the second," she said. "He lost his son and never met his grandchildren, over half a verse." One boy asked whether she hated him. Bernice shook her head. "I prayed for him for years. When he was dying, he asked Thomas to come. He said he was sorry. Thomas forgave him in that hospital room, and I did too, outside in the hall, by myself." She folded the letter. "Children, here is what I want you to know. The Bible guards marriage. It tells you to marry someone who loves the Lord. But it never once told anybody to marry by color. When people use the Bible to build a wall the Bible did not build, somebody always ends up crying at a wedding nobody came to." Kendra asked the group to pray for families still divided that way. They prayed for a long time.',
