    'Price’s lines are from Vols. 1 and 2. His statement that religion has been the most flagrant perpetrator of racism in the world is his charge, and he defines it carefully: religion as men’s distortion of the Word, not the Word. The 246 years are his count from 1619 to 1865, and the dates are public record. The Good Samaritan clause about neutrality opens a quotation from Maurice I. Irvin (Grace and Race, Alliance Life), a white writer whom Price cites, and is attributed to Irvin; the sentence about silence is Price’s. The Slave Bible was printed in England for the British West Indies; it is cited as a record of the same distortion, not as an American edition. Price himself names the genuine Christians who opposed slavery, and so does this lesson.',
