      title: 'The Missing Pages',
      body: 'For Black History Month the youth group at Bethel AME drove two hours to see an exhibit on Bibles that were made for enslaved people. Brother Curtis Ward, who led the trip, had told them only that it would be hard. In the glass case lay a small brown book, open to its title page. A placard explained that the book had been printed in London for the enslaved people of the British West Indies, and that whole parts of the Bible had been cut out of it. The Exodus was gone. Most of the prophets were gone. Fifteen-year-old Jalen read the list of missing verses on the placard and stopped at one. "They took out the verse about stealing a man," he said. Curtis nodded and opened his own Bible to it on the bench: "And he that stealeth a man, and selleth him, or if he be found in his hand, he shall surely be put to death." (Exodus 21:16) The group was quiet. A girl named Precious asked the question everybody was thinking. "So the Bible was on our side the whole time?" Curtis took his time answering. "The Word was always on the side of the oppressed," he said. "Men cut out what they did not want people to hear. That is not the Word. That is religion pretending to be the Word." On the ride home they sang, the way their great-great-grandparents had sung about Moses and Pharaoh, songs the masters could not cut out because they were carried in memory, not in print. Jalen sat by the window and wrote in his phone the missing verses, one by one. "I want to know everything they tried to hide," he said. Curtis smiled for the first time all day. "That is the best answer to the whole exhibit," he said. "Read the whole Book."',
