      body: 'Dave Lindgren had coached Little League in the same suburban park for twelve years, and he had never once said anything when the other dads made jokes. They were not his jokes, he told himself. He laughed politely or looked at his phone. Then his team got a new shortstop, a quick, cheerful eleven-year-old named Marcus Bell, and one Saturday, in the bleachers, Dave heard one of those jokes about Marcus’s family. Marcus’s father, Andre, heard it too. Andre did not say anything; he just looked at the field. Dave went home and could not sleep. On Sunday his pastor happened to preach on the Good Samaritan, and the last words of the story landed on him like a fly ball: "Go, and do thou likewise" (Luke 10:37). He realized that for twelve years he had been the one who passed by on the other side. The next Saturday he asked the dads to stay after the game. His hands were shaking. "I have laughed at things I should have stopped," he said. "I am done with that, and I am asking you to be done with it too. These kids are watching us." Two of the men looked embarrassed. One got angry and left. Most of them nodded. Then Dave walked over to Andre Bell. "I owe you an apology," he said. "I heard it last week and said nothing. That was wrong." Andre was quiet a moment, then shook his hand. "Thank you for saying it out loud," he said. "Most people never do." That fall the two families started grilling together after games, and Marcus hit his first home run with both dads yelling from the same bleacher.',
