    'What stands on the text: Matthew 1 names Rahab, a Canaanite of Jericho, and Ruth, a Moabitess, in the line of Jesus, and Genesis 10 makes Canaan a son of Ham and Heth a son of Canaan. Price’s description of Rahab as a black woman is his inference from that lineage; the Word names her people, not her color. Price himself hedges on Tamar and Bathsheba with very possibly and very possible; Genesis 38 does not name Tamar’s people, and 2 Samuel 11:3 names Bathsheba’s father as Eliam, which points toward an Israelite family if he is the Eliam of 2 Samuel 23:34. The Word gives no description of the earthly appearance of Jesus; historians agree He was a Jewish man of first-century Galilee. Price’s point that pictures of a white Jesus fed the lie of white supremacy is his argument and is taught as his.',
