      title: 'The Picture in the Hallway',
      body: 'For forty years a painting of Jesus hung in the hallway of Calvary Missionary Baptist Church, right outside the fellowship hall: a gentle man with long light-brown hair and blue eyes, holding a lamb. Nobody remembered who had donated it. When the church renovated, the trustees argued for three meetings about what to hang there instead. Deacon Harold wanted the old picture back. Sister Lorraine wanted a painting of a Black Jesus she had seen in Atlanta. The youth pastor, Brother Andre, finally asked if he could try something different. On the Sunday the hallway reopened, the congregation found no painting at all. Instead there was a long wooden board with names carved into it, one after another, starting with Abraham and ending with Jesus. Five names were painted gold: Tamar, Rahab, Ruth, the wife of Uriah, and Mary. Under the board, a small card printed the verse: "And Salmon begat Booz of Rachab; and Booz begat Obed of Ruth" (Matthew 1:5). The children loved it. They ran their fingers along the names and asked who each woman was. Andre told them Rahab came from Jericho, a Canaanite city, and Ruth came from Moab, and both of them chose to trust Yahweh. "So Jesus’ family had people from different nations in it?" a little girl asked. "That is exactly right," Andre said. "The Bible never tells us what color His skin was. It tells us whose family He came from, and it is a family big enough for everybody." Deacon Harold stood in front of the board a long time after service. Then he said to Lorraine, "I think I like this better than either picture." She laughed. "Me too, Harold. Me too."',
