    'Price questioned the popular word reconciliation. To reconcile, by the dictionary, is to restore a friendship, and Black and white Americans, in the nation and in the Church, had never been accepted as equals or friends. "Only equals can be reconciled." The first step, he said, is not reconciliation but recognition: the white church accepting once and for all that Acts 17:26 means what it says. And because racism had been practiced in public, it had to be confronted in public, as Paul confronted Peter at Antioch (Galatians 2:11-14), always with the door open and with love as the motive.',
