      title: 'The Unity Service',
      body: 'For eleven years First Baptist downtown and Greater Bethel across the tracks held a Unity Sunday every January. The white choir sang, the Black choir sang, the two pastors hugged on the platform, and everyone went home to separate lunches. This year Pastor Leon Harris of Greater Bethel asked to meet his friend Pastor Mark Ellison beforehand. "Mark, I love you," he said. "But I can’t do the hug this year. Not like this." Mark was hurt. "Leon, what did I do?" Leon slid a folder across the table. Inside were two pages: the county’s list of foreclosures in the Bethel neighborhood after a lender targeted it, and the minutes of a First Baptist business meeting from 1964 voting to keep Black visitors from joining. "We have been hugging for eleven years," Leon said, "and nobody has ever said one word about either page. You can’t restore a friendship that never started. We have to start with the truth." Mark read the pages for a long time. Then he did something he had never done. He took them to his deacons and read them aloud. Some of the men were angry. One old deacon, who had been at that 1964 meeting, put his face in his hands and wept. In January, Unity Sunday looked different. Pastor Ellison stood up first and read the 1964 vote to both congregations. Then he read Paul’s words about Peter at Antioch, "I said unto Peter before them all" (Galatians 2:14), and said, "What was done before all must be confessed before all." He asked forgiveness. Leon walked across the platform and did not hug him. He shook his hand, looked him in the eye, and said, "Brother." Afterward the two churches ate lunch together for the first time. It was awkward. It was also the first real meal.',
