      title: 'Equals at the Table',
      body: 'At Crossroads Community Church, the men’s breakfast had two tables, and nobody had planned it that way. The Black brothers sat by the window and the white brothers sat by the kitchen, and that was just how it went, Saturday after Saturday. Then a young man named Isaiah Brooks, newly back from the Marines, walked in, looked at the room, and laughed out loud. "Y’all know this looks like a cafeteria in 1962, right?" Everybody laughed, but it stung. Brother Ray Kowalski, who ran the breakfast, took it to heart. The next Saturday he did not move anybody. He just put up a sign: Bring your Bible and sit next to someone you do not know. Then he read the verse he had been chewing on all week: "For he is our peace, who hath made both one, and hath broken down the middle wall of partition between us" (Ephesians 2:14). "If Jesus tore it down," Ray said, "I don’t want to be the one who keeps building it back with folding tables." Isaiah ended up next to old Mr. Hendricks, a retired steelworker who had not said much to anyone in years. They found out they had both served overseas and both lost a brother young. By the fourth Saturday, the two of them were running the coffee together and arguing about football. Ray noticed something else. When the men prayed now, they prayed for each other’s families by name. Isaiah’s mother got sick that winter, and the first casserole at her door came from Mrs. Hendricks. "That," Ray told the pastor, "is when I knew we were finally brothers, not just neighbors at a breakfast."',
